<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thaenae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thyna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thaenae was an ancient port town on the eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, just south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a>, at the site now called Thyna. It marked the eastern end of the fossa regia, the ditch separating the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from Numidia, and later lay in the province of Byzacena. Excavations have revealed baths, houses and mosaics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thaenae.jpg" alt="Thaenae" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Golli Mohamed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thyna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thaenae lay in or near Thyna, now a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Its founders gave it the Punic name tʿynt, which Barclay V. Head transcribed as Thainath. Greek writers rendered it Thaína and Thenae, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ones Thenae, Thaena and Thaenae. Strabo called the town Thena, and Ptolemy gave it both as Thaina and Theaenae. In a later period it became a Roman colony with the name Aelia Augusta Mercurialis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town began as a Phoenician colony and, with the rest of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, passed under Carthaginian and then Roman control in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>. Around the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and Augustus it struck its own bronze coins, showing a female head, taken to be either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, and on the reverse a temple of four columns, with the town's name in Punic letters. The surviving ruins include a bath house, a wealthy townhouse, city walls, housing of poorer people and an early Christian basilica.</p><p>In late antiquity Thenae was the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and a life of St Fulgentius records a council held there. Six bishops are known. Eucrazio attended the council of 256 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> called by St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprian">Cyprian</a>. A Catholic bishop, Latonio, and a Donatist rival, Securo, both appeared at the council of 411 in Carthage. Pascasio took part in the synod of 484 summoned by the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a> and was afterwards exiled. Pontian spoke at the council of 525, and Felix attended the antimonotelite council of 646.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins of the ancient town stand at Thyna, on the edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a>. Thenae survives as a titular see of the Roman Catholic Church, whose holders since 1906 have included Andrzej Maria Deskur and Marian Duś, a former auxiliary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thenae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Thysdrus, with its great amphitheatre at El Djem, Sufetula and <b>Thaenae</b> flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzacena">Byzacena</a></li></ul></section>
